ben.support: Escalating a customer issue — Node/TS SDK streaming on edge runtime: first byte of SSE/ReadableStream delayed ~8-12s, then stream flows normally. Happens on Vercel and Cloudflare edge. SDK version 1.6.2. Customer saw 401 then retry -> 200 but with big initial delay. Repro attached.
alice.dev: ack. can you paste the minimal repro and request_id? do we have a cURL or node snippet? also which node version on their end?
ben.support: repro (sanitized) below and request_id: rq_0bC3fL9
```js
// minimal repro using @redwood/inference-js 1.6.2
const res = await client.chat.completions.stream({
  model: 'redwood-3b',
  messages: [{role:'user', content:'hi'}],
});
const reader = res.body.getReader();
const start = Date.now();
while(true){
  const { value, done } = await reader.read();
  if(done) break;
  console.log('chunk at', Date.now()-start, 'ms', new TextDecoder().decode(value));
}
```
ben.support: Logs from customer: "first chunk at 8200ms -> then 50-80ms per subsequent chunk". Node 18.16 on Vercel edge runtime. They turned off retries in SDK and still saw delay. Upgraded to 1.7.0 on a branch, no change.
edge-bot: Incident detector: possible increased TTFB for streaming endpoints in region eu-west-1 between 2026-03-01 and 2026-03-15. Note: no mass error spike.
carlos.eng: curious — any proxy/ingress logs for rq_0bC3fL9? also did they have any large prompt embeddings/cache warmups? can you dump request headers (no secrets) and most recent route/backends used?
ben.support: attached headers (sanitized):
Authorization: Bearer <redacted>
User-Agent: Redwood-TS-SDK/1.6.2
X-Redwood-Route: eu-west-1-a
sam.sre: I pulled the L7 trace for rq_0bC3fL9: connection established -> backend assigned -> backend sends first chunk after ~8.1s. No 5xx from backend. TCP/SSL handshakes normal. I suspect cold-start or runtime kernel scheduling on some edge hosts.
alice.dev: can you repro from our infra? i tried locally on node 20 and didn't see the delay. can we try against the exact customer's region/route with SDK pinned to 1.6.2 and node 18.16? also try Fetch polyfill vs built-in fetch.
ben.support: customer allowed a test run; they reproduced consistently by hitting our hosted endpoint from Vercel edge-worker. I asked them to run with DEBUG=rw:stream and share timings.
ben.support: DEBUG output:
[rw:stream] open connection
[rw:stream] headers received
[rw:stream] waiting for first chunk
[rw:stream] first-chunk delay 8204ms
[rw:stream] subsequent chunks normal
carlos.eng: thanks. pattern matches a small class of cases where the edge runtime's ReadableStream pipeline delays draining until a minimal-sized chunk is available (some proxies buffer until >N bytes). SSE/text-streams with many tiny chunks can get stuck. Also observed auth rotation interactions where a retry causes the server to sent an initial empty chunk that gets coalesced and delayed by upstream.
carlos.eng: quick triage plan:
- eng-runtime: check if edge host's L7 proxy coalesces small payloads for certain transfer-encodings.
- devex: we can ship an SDK workaround to ensure we send a 1-byte preface immediately (e.g., send "\n" before payload) for streaming responses when using edge runtimes.
alice.dev: we should also confirm whether disabling chunked compression or setting Transfer-Encoding: chunked affects behavior. sam, can you confirm proxy flags for eu-west-1-a?
sam.sre: proxy has default tcp_nodelay true and no custom coalescing flag, but our edge vendor mentioned they sometimes coalesce very small writes for SSE on older kernel builds. filing vendor ticket.
ben.support: customer tried a hack in their worker: they read one byte from the stream then waited; this removed the big pause (first byte came immediately). so workaround on their side is possible but awkward.
carlos.eng: proposed immediate-1byte workaround in SDK (short patch) — when creating the readable stream response, enqueue a single "\n" before any model tokens so proxies flush. patch ready in internal branch; will canary to hosted in next 48h. for SDK, recommended snippet below as temporary workaround:
```js
// SDK workaround for consumers (client-side): force a tiny keepalive preface
const res = await client.chat.completions.stream(...);
const reader = res.body.getReader();
// read first small chunk fast to unblock proxy
const { value } = await reader.read();
if(value && value.length === 1){
  // likely preface, continue normally
}
```
carlos.eng: long-term fix: runtime will adjust write behavior to emit an explicit preface chunk when streaming via SSE/ReadableStream, and we'll add a proxy rule to avoid coalescing small first-chunks. canary rollout planned to eu-west-1-a then global. estimated rollout: 48-96h depending on vendor timeline.
alice.dev: can we also add an SDK config flag like `streamingPreface: true|false` to toggle in future minor so customers can opt-in until patch lands?
carlos.eng: yes, adding flag to 1.7.x line. we'll include docs and a migration note.
ben.support: passing workaround and ETA to customer. they asked about billing impact — they saw no extra token billing; just latency complaints.
alice.dev: follow-up items:
- sam.sre: vendor ticket + trace link
- carlos.eng: create canary PR and link to runtime change
- devex: draft SDK migration doc and temporary flag
- ben.support: update customer with workaround + ETA
ben.support: thanks team :thumbsup:
edge-bot: Action logged: escalation-2478901234 assigned to eng-runtime; priority P2."
